# config/kernel/Kconfig.panic — Panic policy and crash dump

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Panic policy (RT7)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Panic policy"
    default PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN if ROBOT_DRONE
    default PANIC_POLICY_RESET
    help
      Which kernel panics stop the machine. The panic action above applies
      to every panic that takes the reset path.

      Ground robots reset (halt or reboot) on any kernel panic. A flying
      robot with armed ESCs must not: a motor stop in flight is the failure.
      The Robot drone profile (ROBOT_DRONE) therefore defaults to Contain.

    config PANIC_POLICY_RESET
        bool "Reset (every panic: safe state, then halt or reboot)"
        help
          Every kernel panic stops all actuators (lock-free), sets the global
          panic flag (every other hart halts at its next tick and stops
          feeding the watchdog), writes the crash record (RAM pstore and
          /fat/CRASH.LOG) and takes the panic action. The behaviour before
          RT7.

    config PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN
        bool "Contain (park the culprit task; the control loop keeps running)"
        help
          "Warn, don't kill." A panic in a non-safety kernel task is
          contained when its context passes the predicate in
          crates/core/common/src/panic_policy.rs: not in an interrupt
          handler, no SpinLock held by the hart, interrupts were enabled, no
          Mutex held by the task, not a second panic, no hart already on
          the reset path, and the task is neither idle, nor ring 3 in a
          system call, nor a safety task (rt-motor, flight-ctrl). The task is
          parked (it exits with status 134 and its resources are released by
          the ordinary exit path); the global panic flag is NOT set, the
          actuators are NOT stopped, the other harts keep running and the
          crash counter (boot-loop detection) is not raised. The record still
          goes to the RAM pstore and /fat/CRASH.LOG, marked CONTAINED. The
          flight controller then flies its own Land and, after
          PANIC_CONTAIN_LAND_MS, Disarm; it never resets.

          Every panic that fails the predicate takes the reset path, as
          under Reset, and prints the reason. A fault on the control loop
          itself is one of those: the reset path stops feeding the watchdog
          and the external ESC/FC failsafe is the backstop.

          Cost (QEMU defconfigs, release, measured 2026-10-03): kernel
          .text +1742 B on riscv64 and +2128 B on aarch64 over Reset (the
          contain path and flight-ctrl's Land/Disarm sequence). Reset
          itself grew +1326 B / +2176 B over the tree before RT7 (the
          predicate captured on every panic, the safety-task registry,
          the Mutex held count). Every Mutex acquisition and release
          does one more atomic add on a 64-entry per-TID table (+6
          instructions each in riscv64 try_acquire and in the guard's release).

endchoice

config PANIC_CONTAIN_LAND_MS
    int "Contained panic: Land duration before Disarm (ms)"
    range 100 600000
    default 5000
    help
      After a contained kernel panic the flight controller (if armed) flies
      Land for this long, then disarms. There is no touchdown detection in
      the flight controller, so the descent is time-bounded: set it to the
      worst-case time to descend from the vehicle's operating altitude at
      the Land throttle. Read only when PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN is set.

config PANIC_QUIESCE
    bool "Panic: stop the other CPUs and own the console before the report"
    default y
    help
      On the reset path the panicking CPU, right after the actuator stop and
      before the first byte of its report: claims the console (any other
      CPU's UART write then waits until the report is out), rings every
      other online CPU with the scheduler's IPI (its handler parks the CPU,
      as the next timer tick would) and waits up to PANIC_STOP_WAIT_US for
      them to park. The report then says "[PANIC] other CPUs parked: n/m".
      A second CPU panicking meanwhile parks instead of printing.

      Without it the other CPUs keep running until their next tick (or a
      tickless sleep's end) and their console output splices the report
      byte by byte: a garbled last message from the board, and gate rows
      that read it fail.

      Cost: one load and a branch in the IPI handler on every ISA (the
      cross-CPU wake path), one relaxed load per UART write call, and on a
      panic at most PANIC_STOP_WAIT_US. Same default on every ISA, profile
      and mode: a crash report is worth more than either. Turn it off only
      to reproduce the old behaviour.

config PANIC_STOP_WAIT_US
    int "Panic: how long to wait for the other CPUs to park (us)"
    range 0 1000000
    default 100000 if BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default 10000
    help
      The longest the panicking CPU waits for the other online CPUs to take
      its stop IPI and park (PANIC_QUIESCE) before it prints anyway. A CPU
      takes the IPI as soon as it unmasks interrupts, so the wait is the
      longest interrupts-masked window on any CPU; on a board 10 ms is far
      above every measured one (microseconds) and invisible next to the
      report itself. Under QEMU each CPU is a host thread that a loaded host
      can deschedule for tens of milliseconds (measured 2026-10-10: one boot
      in twenty of panic-splice-smoke on aarch64 missed 10 ms), so 100 ms in
      a development build (BUILD_TYPE_DEV: the QEMU defconfigs; the build
      type, not the board, keys it).
      Every panic pays the whole wait when a CPU counted online never runs
      (NUM_ONLINE_CPUS is a prefix bound) or keeps interrupts masked on
      purpose. Linux waits up to 1 s (smp_send_stop); raise it on a board
      whose masked windows are long. Read only when PANIC_QUIESCE is set.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Crash dump of a ring-3 task (owner decision 16-10: Log + file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config CRASHDUMP
    # cargo-feature: crashdump
    bool "Crash dump: a minidump of every ring-3 task a fault kills"
    default n if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default y
    help
      A ring-3 task that a fault kills (page fault it cannot resolve, illegal
      instruction, misaligned or bus error) leaves a minidump: the registers
      of the trap, a window of its stack from the stack pointer, its maps
      (the runs of mapped pages and their permissions: the executable run
      that holds the pc gives the load address of the image), the last
      decision records of the kernel, and the SHA-256 of its image, the key
      of the symbol file of that exact build. It goes to the Log and a file:

      * the Log: a record of the task and the status on the flight
        recorder's ring (it survives a reset), written on the fault path
        with no I/O and no allocation;
      * a file, CRASHn.DMP in the root of the first write tree (`Cap<Dir>`)
        the task's Domain holds, staged in the crash ring in the kernel on
        the fault path (CRASHDUMP_RING dumps, the oldest overwritten) and
        written by the log flusher task, never by the faulting task (an RT
        task never does block I/O) and never by a global path. The files are
        a ring of CRASHDUMP_FILES slots, so a crash loop cannot fill the
        volume. A Domain whose row holds no write tree leaves the flight
        recorder's record only: its dump is not encoded, since nothing
        reads a dump that is not a file.

      `why crash <tid>` prints a dump (userspace/services/why), reading the
      files beneath the read-only root directory its row holds. A kernel
      panic is not a dump: it keeps /fat/CRASH.LOG.

      Cost: CRASHDUMP_RING x (a few KiB) of .bss, one page-table walk and
      one fixed-size copy per killed task. Nothing on any other path.

config CRASHDUMP_STACK_BYTES
    int "Crash dump: stack window (bytes, from the stack pointer up)"
    depends on CRASHDUMP
    range 64 4096
    default 512 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 1024
    help
      The stack bytes a dump keeps, from the stack pointer toward the stack
      top, stopping at the first page that is not mapped. Enough for the
      innermost frames and their saved return addresses.

config CRASHDUMP_MAPS
    int "Crash dump: map runs kept"
    depends on CRASHDUMP
    range 4 48
    default 24
    help
      Runs of mapped pages (same permissions, adjacent) a dump lists, lowest
      address first. A task with more has the count of all of them in the
      dump and the lowest ones listed.

config CRASHDUMP_DECISIONS
    int "Crash dump: decision records kept"
    depends on CRASHDUMP
    range 0 32
    default 16
    help
      The last decision records (crates/core/decision) about the dying task
      a dump carries: why the kernel refused or denied what that thread or
      its process asked for before the fault. Only records whose subject is
      a TID naming one of them: the dump is written into the task's own
      tree. Empty unless DECISION_RECORDS_DEV or DECISION_REFUSALS_DURABLE
      is on. 0 leaves them out.

config CRASHDUMP_RING
    int "Crash dump: crash ring depth (dumps kept in RAM)"
    depends on CRASHDUMP
    range 1 8
    default 2
    help
      Dumps the kernel stages until the log flusher has written them to their
      file. A fault while the ring is full overwrites the oldest dump that
      was already written, else the oldest one; the drop is counted.

config CRASHDUMP_FILES
    int "Crash dump: file slots per Domain directory"
    depends on CRASHDUMP
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      Dump files CRASH0.DMP .. CRASHn.DMP a Domain's tree holds.
      The oldest is rewritten; `why crash` reads them all.
